/* Chapter 18 clinical case cards: single best answer. Source: Kaplan & Sadock's Synopsis of Psychiatry, 12th ed., Chapter 18. */
KS.add("ch18", "cases", [
  {"id":"c18-in01","tag":"Presentation","q":"A 30-year-old man describes a rising sense of tension before he acts on an urge he knows is harmful. Right after acting he feels relieved. Hours later he feels guilt, self-reproach, and dread. Which feature typically follows the act immediately in this class of disorders?","choices":["Mounting anger","Dissociative amnesia","Immediate gratification and relief","Persistent anticipatory pleasure","Sustained euphoria for days"],"answer":2,"why":"Completing the act brings immediate gratification and relief; remorse, guilt, self-reproach and dread follow after a variable time (Clinical presentation, s18-intro)."},
  {"id":"c18-in02","tag":"Presentation","q":"A 45-year-old woman finally discloses a decades-long pattern of a harmful repeated act. She says she hid it from everyone, including her doctors. Which feature of impulse-control disorders best explains her late presentation?","choices":["Shameful secretiveness that spreads through her life","Consistent anticipatory pleasure","Lack of any distress","Absence of tension before acts","Planned involvement of others"],"answer":0,"why":"Shameful secretiveness about the repeated activity often permeates the whole life and delays treatment (Clinical presentation)."},
  {"id":"c18-in03","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 28-year-old man with a long record of deceit and disregard for others is evaluated. The team notes DSM-5 lists his likely diagnosis both in the disruptive, impulse-control, and conduct disorders class and in another section. Which diagnosis is this?","choices":["Pyromania","Kleptomania","Antisocial personality disorder","Intermittent explosive disorder","Oppositional defiant disorder"],"answer":2,"why":"DSM-5 includes antisocial personality disorder both in this class and among personality disorders; it is covered in Chapter 19 (s18-intro)."},
  {"id":"c18-in04","tag":"Presentation","q":"A 33-year-old woman with a repetitive harmful impulse says she does not look forward to the act and feels no pleasure beforehand, only rising tension. Her clinician doubts an impulse-control disorder. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["Anticipatory pleasure is present only sometimes","Anticipatory pleasure must be present","Remorse afterward excludes the diagnosis","Relief after the act excludes the diagnosis","Tension rules out an impulse-control disorder"],"answer":0,"why":"Mounting tension and arousal is usual, only sometimes and not consistently mixed with conscious anticipatory pleasure (Clinical presentation)."},
  {"id":"c18-ie01","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 36-year-old real estate agent loses clients and girlfriends because of sudden rages, sometimes throwing his desk and books, often with no apparent conflict. Between episodes he is kind and has many friends. He has no medical or neurologic problems and denies mood symptoms or antisocial activity. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pyromania","Bipolar disorder, manic episode","Kleptomania","Intermittent explosive disorder","Antisocial personality disorder"],"answer":3,"why":"Discrete, disproportionate aggressive episodes with no aggression between episodes and no better explanation fit intermittent explosive disorder (real estate agent case, s18-ied)."},
  {"id":"c18-ie02","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 25-year-old man had one violent outburst last month, his first ever, after an insult. He has no other history of aggression. He asks whether he has intermittent explosive disorder. What is the best answer?","choices":["Yes, if it lasted under 30 minutes","No, because there was provocation","Yes, one disproportionate episode is enough","Yes, if he felt guilt afterward","No, one discrete episode does not justify the diagnosis"],"answer":4,"why":"History must reveal several episodes of loss of control; one discrete episode does not justify the diagnosis (Diagnosis, s18-ied)."},
  {"id":"c18-ie03","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 5-year-old boy has frequent explosive tantrums with hitting, several times a week for 4 months. His developmental level is age-appropriate. Why can intermittent explosive disorder not be diagnosed now?","choices":["Tantrums never count as aggression","He has not had 3 damaging episodes","Duration must be 12 months for all symptoms","He is younger than the required age of 6 years","ICD-10 does not allow it in boys"],"answer":3,"why":"A relatively unique requirement is age above 6 years (or comparable developmental level); Table 18-1 excludes age under 6."},
  {"id":"c18-ie04","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 30-year-old woman has verbal outbursts out of proportion to provocation, without damage or injury, about twice a week for the past 3 months. She is distressed and her work suffers. No medical, substance, or other psychiatric cause is found. Which DSM-5 criterion pattern does she meet?","choices":["Symptom 2, damaging outbursts 3 times in a year","ICD-10 symptoms only","None, because she has distress","None, because no property was damaged","Symptom 1, nondamaging aggression twice a week for 3 months"],"answer":4,"why":"Table 18-1: symptom 1 (aggression not causing damage) requires an average of twice a week for 3 months; either symptom 1 or 2 suffices."},
  {"id":"c18-ie05","tag":"Workup","q":"A 40-year-old man with new episodes of disproportionate aggression is referred. Before diagnosing intermittent explosive disorder, which step is most appropriate?","choices":["Wait for a fourth episode","Start a long-acting antipsychotic","Exclude medical illness, substances and other psychiatric disorders with history and tests","Diagnose at once since aggression is disproportionate","Refer for neurosurgery"],"answer":2,"why":"Table 18-1 excludes aggression resulting from medical illness, substance, or another psychiatric disorder; blood chemistry, urinalysis with drug toxicology and syphilis serology help rule out other causes (s18-ied, s18-other-tests)."},
  {"id":"c18-ie06","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 29-year-old man punches walls only while acutely intoxicated with a substance, and never when sober. Which DSM-5 exclusion argues against intermittent explosive disorder?","choices":["The outbursts result from a substance","Lack of damage","Presence of regret afterward","Age under 6","Duration less than 30 minutes"],"answer":0,"why":"Table 18-1 lists substance as an exclusion: the aggression must not be the result of a substance."},
  {"id":"c18-ie07","tag":"Presentation","q":"A 34-year-old man with intermittent explosive disorder describes his childhood. Which background is most typical?","choices":["A calm, structured home with no conflict","Early fascination with fire","A home with alcohol use disorder, violence and emotional instability","Stealing unneeded objects since childhood","Repeated head injuries from sports"],"answer":2,"why":"Histories typically describe a childhood in an atmosphere of alcohol use disorder, violence, and emotional instability (Diagnosis, s18-ied)."},
  {"id":"c18-ie08","tag":"Presentation","q":"A 38-year-old man has recurrent rages that start suddenly and are over within minutes. What else is most typical of his history?","choices":["Steady employment and stable marriage","Job losses, marital problems, legal trouble, and past psychiatric help to no avail","Careful planning of each outburst","Persistent aggression every day between episodes","No prior help-seeking"],"answer":1,"why":"Work histories are poor with job losses, marital difficulties and trouble with the law; most have sought psychiatric help before without success (s18-ied)."},
  {"id":"c18-ie09","tag":"Presentation","q":"A 36-year-old man with intermittent explosive disorder is started on medication and psychotherapy. Over months his outbursts lessen. In the guide's case of the real estate agent, what change in the patient explained the improvement?","choices":["He moved away from his family","He began daily exercise only","He became unable to feel anger","He stopped all contact with clients","He became aware of early signs of losing control and avoided confrontation"],"answer":4,"why":"In the real estate agent case, outbursts improved as he became aware of early warning signs and learned techniques to avoid confrontation (s18-ied)."},
  {"id":"c18-ie10","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 27-year-old woman's outbursts damaged property 3 times this year. A clinician asks how ICD-10 classifies this condition. Which is correct?","choices":["As other habit and impulse disorders, not specifically defined","As a substance-related disorder","As a specific disorder with defined criteria","As a mood disorder","As a personality disorder"],"answer":0,"why":"Table 18-1: ICD-10 names it other habit and impulse disorders (intermittent explosive disorder) and does not specifically define it."},
  {"id":"c18-kl01","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 42-year-old wealthy executive repeatedly shoplifts cheap underwear she considers tacky, never wears it, and stores it in boxes. She could easily afford it. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Kleptomania","Intermittent explosive disorder","Antisocial personality disorder","Conduct disorder","Pyromania"],"answer":0,"why":"Recurrent stealing of unneeded, valueless items she could afford, kept hidden, fits kleptomania; the act matters, not the object (Ms. J's case, s18-klepto)."},
  {"id":"c18-kl02","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 26-year-old man steals from his former employer to get back at him for firing him. He feels angry while stealing. Why is this not kleptomania?","choices":["He felt tension beforehand","He was alone","The theft is driven by anger and vengeance","He took items he did not need","He did not plan it"],"answer":2,"why":"Kleptomania is not associated with anger or vengeance; Table 18-2 lists both as exclusions."},
  {"id":"c18-kl03","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 24-year-old woman steals impulsively only during a week of decreased need for sleep, grandiosity, and pressured speech. Which DSM-5 exclusion in Table 18-2 applies?","choices":["Mania","Vengeance","Conduct disorder","Psychosis","Antisocial personality disorder"],"answer":0,"why":"Table 18-2 lists mania among the DSM-5 exclusions for kleptomania; the stealing must not be the result of mania."},
  {"id":"c18-kl04","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 35-year-old man steals only when command hallucinations tell him to. What is the best reason not to diagnose kleptomania?","choices":["The items are unneeded","He steals alone","He avoids security guards","The stealing results from psychosis","He feels guilt afterward"],"answer":3,"why":"Psychosis is a DSM-5 exclusion for kleptomania in Table 18-2."},
  {"id":"c18-kl05","tag":"Presentation","q":"A 31-year-old woman with kleptomania is asked what she does after stealing. Which answer is most typical?","choices":["Sells the items for profit","Shares them with accomplices who planned the theft","Destroys them in anger","Gives them away, returns them secretly, or hides them","Uses them daily"],"answer":3,"why":"After taking objects, patients often give them away, return them secretly, or hide them (s18-klepto)."},
  {"id":"c18-kl06","tag":"Presentation","q":"A 39-year-old man with kleptomania does not steal when a security guard is watching, yet has been arrested many times. Which statement best describes his risk behavior?","choices":["He wants to be caught","He plans thefts carefully with others","He avoids immediate arrest but does not rationally weigh the overall risk","He steals for revenge on stores","He has no awareness of arrest at all"],"answer":2,"why":"Patients are unlikely to steal when immediate arrest is probable, but may not rationally consider the risks despite repeated, humiliating arrests (s18-klepto)."},
  {"id":"c18-kl07","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 17-year-old boy steals cars with friends, sells parts, and has a long pattern of violating others' rights since age 12. Which DSM-5 exclusion makes kleptomania unlikely?","choices":["Conduct disorder","Psychosis","Age under 6","Mania","Substance"],"answer":0,"why":"Conduct disorder is a DSM-5 exclusion for kleptomania (Table 18-2); kleptomanic thefts are also unplanned and do not involve others."},
  {"id":"c18-kl08","tag":"Presentation","q":"A 42-year-old executive with kleptomania becomes significantly depressed and drinks heavily. After another arrest she makes a suicidal gesture. In the guide's case of Ms. J, which treatment did she agree to?","choices":["Lithium and valproate","Electroconvulsive therapy","Long-term hospitalization","Carbamazepine alone","Citalopram and naltrexone"],"answer":4,"why":"Ms. J consented to citalopram and naltrexone and credited her AA group as at least as effective (Ms. J's case, s18-klepto)."},
  {"id":"c18-kl09","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 32-year-old man repeatedly steals because his friends dare him, and they plan the thefts together. Which feature argues most against kleptomania?","choices":["The thefts are planned and involve others","He sometimes feels guilt","He feels tension before stealing","He feels relief afterward","The items have little value"],"answer":0,"why":"In kleptomania the theft is not planned and does not involve others (s18-klepto)."},
  {"id":"c18-kl10","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 30-year-old man has stolen for years. He feels no guilt or remorse afterward, but he does feel tension before and relief after, with no anger, and the items are unneeded. A colleague says kleptomania needs guilt. What is correct?","choices":["Lack of guilt means antisocial personality disorder","Kleptomania requires guilt","He must also feel vengeance","Guilt is required only in ICD-10","Guilt or remorse may or may not occur in kleptomania"],"answer":4,"why":"Patients may or may not feel guilt or remorse; the core is tension before and gratification or relief after, without anger or vengeance (s18-klepto)."},
  {"id":"c18-py01","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 22-year-old man has set several fires in empty lots. He feels mounting arousal beforehand, relief while watching firefighters work, volunteers at the fire station, and gains nothing financially. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pyromania","Kleptomania","Arson","Intermittent explosive disorder","Antisocial personality disorder"],"answer":0,"why":"Repeated deliberate fire setting with arousal before, gratification watching the aftermath, firefighting interest, and no personal benefit fits pyromania (s18-pyro)."},
  {"id":"c18-py02","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 45-year-old businessman sets fire to his failing restaurant to collect insurance. Which term best fits the behavior?","choices":["Pyromania","Kleptomania","Other specified impulse-control disorder","Arson","Intermittent explosive disorder"],"answer":3,"why":"In pyromania there is no personal benefit, financial or otherwise; personal benefit marks the distinction from arson (s18-pyro)."},
  {"id":"c18-py03","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 19-year-old woman has frequent vivid fantasies of setting buildings on fire and feels drawn to fire, but has never set one. Which statement is correct?","choices":["Fantasy alone qualifies if repetitive","She meets criteria for arson","Pyromania requires actually setting fires","Pyromania requires a financial motive","She meets criteria for pyromania"],"answer":2,"why":"To be diagnosed with pyromania, patients must set fires, not just desire or imagine them (s18-pyro)."},
  {"id":"c18-py04","tag":"Presentation","q":"A 27-year-old man with repeated fire setting shows no remorse and seems indifferent to damage to life and property. Which other behavior is most typical of pyromania?","choices":["Setting fires only for revenge","Avoiding fire scenes entirely","Setting off false alarms and leaving apparent clues","Stealing objects he does not need","Rage outbursts lasting under 30 minutes"],"answer":2,"why":"Patients with pyromania may set off false alarms and frequently leave apparent clues; they show no remorse and may be indifferent to consequences (s18-pyro)."},
  {"id":"c18-py05","tag":"Presentation","q":"A 24-year-old man who sets fires describes how he spends days gathering materials and choosing a site before each fire. Is this consistent with pyromania?","choices":["No, pyromania is always unplanned","No, preparation means arson","Yes, considerable preparation may occur","No, preparation excludes any impulse-control disorder","No, preparation means conduct disorder"],"answer":2,"why":"Patients with pyromania may make considerable preparations before starting a fire; the act is deliberate and purposeful (s18-pyro)."},
  {"id":"c18-py06","tag":"Presentation","q":"A 23-year-old man sets fires repeatedly and feels pleasure afterward. When does his gratification most typically occur?","choices":["While witnessing or taking part in the aftermath of the fire","Only when he gains money","Never; he feels only remorse","Only days later when reading news","Only before the fire"],"answer":0,"why":"After setting the fire, they feel pleasure, gratification or relief when witnessing or participating in the aftermath (s18-pyro)."},
  {"id":"c18-py07","tag":"Presentation","q":"A 26-year-old man with pyromania tells you he loves fire engines and the gear firefighters use. How is this best understood?","choices":["Attraction to firefighting related to the same gratification","A sign of arson","Evidence against pyromania","An unrelated hobby","A sign of kleptomania"],"answer":0,"why":"Perhaps related to the gratification, they feel a similar attraction to firefighting, including its activities and equipment (s18-pyro)."},
  {"id":"c18-ot01","tag":"Workup","q":"A 32-year-old man presents with new impulsive aggression. Which set of laboratory tests can help rule out other causes?","choices":["Genetic testing","Liver and thyroid function, fasting glucose, electrolytes, urinalysis with drug toxicology, syphilis serology","Lumbar puncture and EEG only","Serum lithium level only","Complete blood count only"],"answer":1,"why":"Blood chemistry (liver and thyroid function, fasting glucose, electrolytes), urinalysis with drug toxicology, and syphilis serology may help rule out other causes (Objective tests)."},
  {"id":"c18-ot02","tag":"Workup","q":"A 41-year-old woman with impulsive behavior has normal blood tests. An MRI is ordered. Changes in which region are associated with loss of impulse control?","choices":["Cerebellum","Brainstem","Occipital cortex","Prefrontal cortex","Spinal cord"],"answer":3,"why":"MRI may reveal changes in the prefrontal cortex, which is associated with loss of impulse control (Objective tests)."},
  {"id":"c18-ot03","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 20-year-old student plays online games 12 hours a day and cannot stop despite failing classes. A resident asks where this condition is best grouped. Which is best?","choices":["Intermittent explosive disorder","Conduct disorder","Pyromania","With the addictive disorders","Kleptomania"],"answer":3,"why":"Internet gaming disorder and related disorders are better considered addictive disorders because their phenomenology, pathology, course and treatment are very similar, though not due to substances (s18-other-tests)."},
  {"id":"c18-ot04","tag":"Workup","q":"A 37-year-old man with episodic aggression has used several street drugs. Which test best checks for a substance explanation?","choices":["Syphilis serology","Thyroid function tests","Fasting blood glucose","Urinalysis with drug toxicology","MRI of the brain"],"answer":3,"why":"Urinalysis including drug toxicology is part of the workup to rule out other causes, and substance is an exclusion in Table 18-1 (s18-other-tests)."},
  {"id":"c18-ot05","tag":"Diagnosis","q":"A 29-year-old woman has a repetitive impulsive behavior causing distress that does not meet criteria for intermittent explosive disorder, kleptomania or pyromania. Which category fits?","choices":["Oppositional defiant disorder","Other specified or unspecified disorder of this class","Conduct disorder","Antisocial personality disorder","No diagnosis is possible"],"answer":1,"why":"Other specified or unspecified disorders serve as a residual category for disorders not meeting criteria for the major disorders in the class (s18-other-tests)."},
  {"id":"c18-ot06","tag":"Workup","q":"A 50-year-old man with new impulsive aggression has a history of untreated sexually transmitted infection. Which test in the standard workup addresses this?","choices":["Thyroid function tests","Syphilis serology","Electrolytes","Liver function tests","Fasting blood glucose"],"answer":1,"why":"Syphilis serology is listed among tests that may help rule out other causes of aggression or impulsive behavior (Objective tests)."},
  {"id":"c18-ic01","tag":"Differential","q":"A 30-year-old man says that for weeks he has felt a rising inner pressure to smash things, yet he has not acted on it once. He asks what to call a tension state that can exist without any action. Which term fits best?","choices":["Command hallucination","Compulsion","Dissociative episode","Ego-dystonic act","Impulse"],"answer":4,"why":"An impulse is a tension state that can exist without an action; a compulsion always has an action component (differential diagnosis section, impulses versus compulsions)."},
  {"id":"c18-ic02","tag":"Differential","q":"A 22-year-old woman with trichotillomania says she feels driven to pull her hair, does not enjoy it and wishes she could stop. How is her experience of the act best described?","choices":["Dissociative amnesia","Larcenous intent","Ego-syntonic and pleasurable","Anticipatory gratification","Ego-dystonic"],"answer":4,"why":"Compulsions, as in OCD and trichotillomania, are often ego-dystonic: the person does not desire or enjoy the act yet feels compelled to do it."},
  {"id":"c18-ic03","tag":"Differential","q":"A 19-year-old student plays video games for hours in a compulsive way and clearly enjoys it. A junior resident argues it cannot be a compulsion because it is pleasurable. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["Compulsions are always ego-dystonic","Any pleasurable repetitive act is an impulse","Pleasure excludes all psychiatric disorders","Compulsions never involve an action","Some compulsions, such as compulsive video game playing, may be pleasurable"],"answer":4,"why":"Not all compulsions are ego-dystonic; compulsive video game playing may have a pleasurable component."},
  {"id":"c18-ic04","tag":"Differential","q":"A 35-year-old woman with an impulse-control disorder says that acting on her urge brings relief, but guilt soon spoils the good feeling. What does this illustrate?","choices":["Her behavior must be a compulsion","Guilt can disturb the sense of pleasure in impulse-control disorders","Impulses never involve pleasure","She is malingering","She has a psychotic disorder"],"answer":1,"why":"Impulses usually aim at pleasure, but impulse-control disorders can also elicit guilt, which disturbs the sense of pleasure."},
  {"id":"c18-ic05","tag":"Differential","q":"A teaching session compares impulsive and compulsive behaviors in a 28-year-old patient. Both are repetitive. Which feature is said to lead to psychosocial impairment?","choices":["A single impulsive act","Any tension state without action","Compulsive behavior in every case","Ego-dystonic feelings alone","Repeated acting out of impulses"],"answer":4,"why":"Both are repetitive, but repeated acting out of impulses leads to psychosocial impairment, whereas compulsive behavior does not always carry that risk."},
  {"id":"c18-ic06","tag":"Differential","q":"A 24-year-old man feels he cannot resist performing a repetitive behavior, describing himself as compelled. Which disorder is named as a compulsive disorder alongside OCD?","choices":["Kleptomania","Pyromania","Trichotillomania","Amok","Intermittent explosive disorder"],"answer":2,"why":"OCD and trichotillomania are given as compulsive disorders in which patients feel compelled to act out the pathologic behavior."},
  {"id":"c18-di01","tag":"Differential","q":"A 32-year-old man has had several sudden violent outbursts out of proportion to provocation. His urine drug screen after the latest episode is positive for amphetamines. Which conclusion is most appropriate?","choices":["Diagnose pyromania","Diagnose amok","Diagnose conduct disorder","Diagnose intermittent explosive disorder now","Intermittent explosive disorder should not be diagnosed until amphetamine intoxication is ruled out as the primary cause"],"answer":4,"why":"Intermittent explosive disorder is diagnosed only after other causes, including intoxication with alcohol, barbiturates, hallucinogens or amphetamines, have been ruled out as the primary cause."},
  {"id":"c18-di02","tag":"Differential","q":"A 16-year-old boy has bullied, fought and threatened others almost daily for years; aggression is a constant part of how he behaves, not separate outbursts. Which diagnosis best explains this pattern?","choices":["Amok","Intermittent explosive disorder","Conduct disorder","Kleptomania","Trichotillomania"],"answer":2,"why":"Conduct disorder and antisocial personality disorder are pervasive patterns in which aggression is a consistent feature, unlike the episodic, discrete outbursts of intermittent explosive disorder."},
  {"id":"c18-di03","tag":"Differential","q":"A 29-year-old man attacks his neighbor, saying voices told him the neighbor was poisoning his food. On examination his reality testing is grossly impaired. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Schizophrenia","Amok","Borderline personality disorder","Kleptomania","Intermittent explosive disorder"],"answer":0,"why":"In schizophrenia, violence occurs in response to delusions and hallucinations, with gross impairment in reality testing."},
  {"id":"c18-di04","tag":"Differential","q":"A 34-year-old woman is brought in after shoving staff. She is hostile and impulsively aggressive, and the mental status examination shows pressured speech, flight of ideas and grandiosity. What is the most likely primary diagnosis?","choices":["Parkinson disease","Intermittent explosive disorder","Pyromania","Malingering","Mania"],"answer":4,"why":"Hostile manic patients may be impulsively aggressive, but the underlying diagnosis is generally apparent from the mental status examination and clinical presentation."},
  {"id":"c18-di05","tag":"Differential","q":"A 27-year-old man from southeastern Asia, with no prior violence, suddenly attacks several people in one acute episode. He appears dissociated and later says he remembers nothing. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pyromania","Intermittent explosive disorder","Amok","Conduct disorder","Antisocial personality disorder"],"answer":2,"why":"Amok is a culture-bound syndrome usually linked to southeastern Asia, with acute violence and claimed amnesia; a single episode and prominent dissociative features separate it from intermittent explosive disorder."},
  {"id":"c18-di06","tag":"Differential","q":"A 66-year-old man with a long-standing neurologic illness develops new impulsive and addictive-like behaviors. Which neurologic disorder is the best-documented mimic of impulse-control disorders?","choices":["Brain tumor","Alzheimer disease","Epilepsy","Parkinson disease","Endocrine disorder"],"answer":3,"why":"Among neurologic disorders, the best-documented examples are with Parkinson disease, which can resemble both impulse-control disorders and addictive disorder."},
  {"id":"c18-di07","tag":"Differential","q":"A 45-year-old man with no past history begins having brief rage episodes. He also reports new morning headaches and a recent first seizure. Which is the best next step?","choices":["Diagnose borderline personality disorder","Reassure and discharge","Diagnose amok","Evaluate for epilepsy and a brain tumor before diagnosing intermittent explosive disorder","Diagnose intermittent explosive disorder"],"answer":3,"why":"Epilepsy, brain tumors, degenerative diseases and endocrine disorders must be ruled out as the primary cause before diagnosing intermittent explosive disorder."},
  {"id":"c18-dk01","tag":"Differential","q":"A 16-year-old girl is caught shoplifting cosmetics with two friends. They say they did it for the thrill and to get the items. She has no psychiatric history. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Antisocial personality disorder","Ordinary shoplifting without a psychiatric disorder","Malingering","Psychotic stealing","Kleptomania"],"answer":1,"why":"Few shoplifters have true kleptomania; most are teenagers and young adults who steal in pairs or small groups for kicks as well as goods and have no psychiatric disorder."},
  {"id":"c18-dk02","tag":"Differential","q":"A 30-year-old man plans thefts in advance, recruits others to steal for him, threatened a guard to escape and shows no remorse. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Amok","Kleptomania","Major depression with psychotic features","Dementia","Antisocial personality disorder"],"answer":4,"why":"Antisocial stealing is for personal gain, planned, often through others, involves threat or violence mainly to elude capture, and lacks guilt and remorse."},
  {"id":"c18-dk03","tag":"Differential","q":"A 78-year-old woman with progressive memory loss walks out of a supermarket holding unpaid items and is confused when stopped. What best explains the theft?","choices":["Forgetfulness from a dementing illness","Command hallucinations","Antisocial personality disorder","Malingering","Kleptomania"],"answer":0,"why":"Patients with Alzheimer disease or other dementing organic illness may leave a store without paying owing to forgetfulness rather than larcenous intent."},
  {"id":"c18-dk04","tag":"Differential","q":"A 40-year-old man with schizophrenia takes items from a shop, explaining that a voice ordered him to collect them. What is the best explanation?","choices":["Dementia","Kleptomania","Malingering","Ordinary shoplifting","Psychotic stealing driven by command hallucinations"],"answer":4,"why":"Theft occasionally occurs during psychotic illness; psychotic stealing is a product of abnormal mood or of command hallucinations or delusions."},
  {"id":"c18-dk05","tag":"Differential","q":"A 25-year-old man with a long record of planned crimes is charged with theft. After his arrest he insists he has kleptomania and cannot resist stealing. Which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Malingering","Kleptomania","Normal adolescent behavior","Dementia","Acute mania"],"answer":0,"why":"People with antisocial personality disorder or no disorder may steal and then claim kleptomania; this is malingering, which can be hard to detect in intelligent perpetrators."},
  {"id":"c18-dk06","tag":"Differential","q":"Parents bring their 5-year-old son because he keeps playing with matches and lighters and watching flames. He has set no fires and has no other problems. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Pyromania","Intellectual disability","Conduct disorder","Schizophrenia","Normal investigation of the environment"],"answer":4,"why":"Many young children are fascinated by matches, lighters and fire as part of the normal investigation of their environment, which should be easy to distinguish from pyromania."},
  {"id":"c18-dk07","tag":"Differential","q":"A 17-year-old boy with a long history of aggression and rule-breaking sets fire to his teacher's car after a failing grade, to get back at her. How is this fire setting best explained?","choices":["Normal fire play","Psychotic fire setting","Pyromania","Failure to appreciate consequences","A deliberate act in conduct disorder"],"answer":4,"why":"In conduct disorder and antisocial personality disorder, fire setting is a deliberate act, for example for retaliation, not a failure to resist an impulse."},
  {"id":"c18-dk08","tag":"Differential","q":"A 22-year-old man with intellectual disability lights paper in a waste bin indoors and seems not to understand why staff are alarmed. What best explains the fire setting?","choices":["Delusions in mania","Pyromania","Failure to appreciate the consequences of the act","Political sabotage","Retaliation in antisocial personality disorder"],"answer":2,"why":"Patients with brain dysfunction (e.g. dementia), intellectual disability (older term: mental retardation) or substance intoxication may set fires because they fail to appreciate the consequences."},
  {"id":"c18-dk09","tag":"Differential","q":"A 35-year-old man sets fire to a government office as a protest for his political movement. What is the best classification of this act?","choices":["Incendiary sabotage by a political extremist","Fire setting from brain dysfunction","Pyromania","Normal fire fascination","Fire setting in response to hallucinations"],"answer":0,"why":"Pyromania must be separated from incendiary acts of sabotage by dissident political extremists or arsonists; sociopolitical ideology is also an exclusion in Table 18-3."},
  {"id":"c18-cm01","tag":"Comorbidity","q":"A 26-year-old man is diagnosed with intermittent explosive disorder. Roughly what share of such patients meet criteria for another psychiatric disorder?","choices":["About 40 percent","Almost none","More than 80 percent","About 25 percent","About 10 percent"],"answer":2,"why":"More than 80 percent of individuals with intermittent explosive disorder meet criteria for another psychiatric disorder (comorbidity section)."},
  {"id":"c18-cm02","tag":"Comorbidity","q":"A 28-year-old woman with kleptomania is being screened for associated conditions. Which eating disorder is especially linked with kleptomania?","choices":["Pica","Anorexia nervosa","Bulimia nervosa","Rumination disorder","Avoidant food intake disorder"],"answer":2,"why":"Kleptomania is associated with eating disorders, especially bulimia nervosa."},
  {"id":"c18-cm03","tag":"Comorbidity","q":"A 20-year-old man with pyromania is assessed for substance use. Which substances are especially associated with pyromania?","choices":["Cocaine and nicotine","Hallucinogens and inhalants","Alcohol and marijuana","Opioids and sedatives","Caffeine and stimulants"],"answer":2,"why":"Pyromania is significantly associated with substance use disorders, especially alcohol and marijuana use disorders."},
  {"id":"c18-cm04","tag":"Comorbidity","q":"A 38-year-old woman with long-standing kleptomania has become depressed and is drinking heavily. Which risk is reported to be higher in kleptomania and needs direct assessment?","choices":["Psychosis","Suicide","Fire setting","Dementia","Amok episodes"],"answer":1,"why":"Individuals with kleptomania are reported to have higher rates of suicide; mood disorders and alcohol use disorder are common comorbidities."},
  {"id":"c18-cm05","tag":"Comorbidity","q":"A 31-year-old man with intermittent explosive disorder is screened for personality pathology. Which personality disorders are listed as comorbid with intermittent explosive disorder?","choices":["Antisocial and borderline","Paranoid and narcissistic","Avoidant and dependent","Schizoid and schizotypal","Obsessive-compulsive and histrionic"],"answer":0,"why":"Comorbid personality disorders in intermittent explosive disorder are antisocial and borderline; these also appear on the rule-out list."},
  {"id":"c18-cm06","tag":"Comorbidity","q":"A 33-year-old woman with kleptomania also reports problems controlling another behavior. Which pair of conditions is associated with kleptomania?","choices":["Pathologic gambling and compulsive shopping","Amok and dissociation","Pyromania and schizophrenia","Tic disorder and stuttering","Parkinson disease and dementia"],"answer":0,"why":"Kleptomania is associated with pathologic gambling and compulsive shopping, as well as eating, personality, OCD and substance use disorders."},
  {"id":"c18-co01","tag":"Course","q":"A 21-year-old man has had several explosive aggressive episodes over the past two years. At what life stage does intermittent explosive disorder usually appear?","choices":["Early childhood","Late adolescence to early adulthood","Only after a head injury","Middle age","Old age"],"answer":1,"why":"Intermittent explosive disorder may begin at any stage of life but usually appears between late adolescence and early adulthood."},
  {"id":"c18-co02","tag":"Course","q":"A 62-year-old man had explosive episodes in his twenties that eased by middle age. After a recent brain injury, episodes return and become frequent and severe. What best explains the change?","choices":["Heightened organic impairment","Amok","Malingering","Onset of kleptomania","Normal course of intermittent explosive disorder"],"answer":0,"why":"Intermittent explosive disorder usually lessens by middle age, but heightened organic impairment can lead to frequent and severe episodes."},
  {"id":"c18-co03","tag":"Course","q":"A 29-year-old woman has stolen unneeded items for years: some months none, other months several episodes, with free spells of weeks. Which description best fits the course of kleptomania?","choices":["Onset in old age","Always remits after the first arrest","Waxes and wanes but tends to be chronic","Single episode with full recovery","Steady daily stealing"],"answer":2,"why":"Kleptomania waxes and wanes but tends to be chronic, ranging from less than one to multiple episodes per month, with free periods of weeks or months."},
  {"id":"c18-co04","tag":"Course","q":"A 34-year-old man with kleptomania had not stolen for a year. After losing his job, he begins stealing again. What does this illustrate?","choices":["His recovery was spontaneous and permanent","Loss or disappointment may precipitate new bouts","He is malingering","He has developed antisocial personality disorder","He has dementia"],"answer":1,"why":"In quiescent cases of kleptomania, loss or disappointment may precipitate new bouts of the disorder."},
  {"id":"c18-co05","tag":"Course","q":"A 40-year-old accountant has had kleptomania for years with no effect on her work or family life. Which event most often brings severe impairment and complications?","choices":["Reaching middle age","Getting married","Moving house","Being caught, especially being arrested","Starting a new job"],"answer":3,"why":"Severe impairment and complications are usually secondary to being caught, particularly being arrested; often the disorder does not impair social or work functioning."},
  {"id":"c18-co06","tag":"Course","q":"A youth fire-setting program reviews risk profiles. In which group is pyromania reported to be more frequent?","choices":["Older adults with dementia","Political extremists","Females with strong social skills","Males with weaker social skills and learning difficulties","Young children exploring fire"],"answer":3,"why":"Pyromania occurs more frequently in males with weaker social skills and learning difficulties (course and prognosis section)."},
  {"id":"c18-co07","tag":"Course","q":"The mother of a 10-year-old boy who has taken items from classmates asks whether he will grow up to have kleptomania. Which reply is most accurate?","choices":["He will certainly develop kleptomania","Most children and adolescents who steal do not become adults with kleptomania","Childhood stealing always means conduct disorder","Kleptomania never begins in childhood","Kleptomania usually begins in middle age"],"answer":1,"why":"Kleptomania may begin in childhood, but most children and adolescents who steal do not become kleptomaniac adults; usual onset is adolescence."},
  {"id":"c18-co08","tag":"Course","q":"A 27-year-old woman with kleptomania asks about her outlook. She has never sought help before and came only after her family insisted. Which statement about prognosis is most accurate?","choices":["Spontaneous recovery is known to be common","The disorder always impairs work functioning","Prognosis is poor even with treatment","Most patients seek help on their own","Prognosis with treatment can be good, but few patients seek help on their own"],"answer":4,"why":"The prognosis with treatment can be good, but few patients come for help of their own accord; the spontaneous recovery rate is unknown."},
  {"id":"c18-co09","tag":"Course","q":"A 24-year-old man began setting fires at 16. His fire setting has come and gone in intensity. What is known about the long-term course of pyromania?","choices":["It is a single-episode disorder","It is proven to worsen in every case","It usually begins after age 50","It always remits by age 30","It is unknown, but smaller studies suggest it may be chronic"],"answer":4,"why":"Most people with pyromania start in adolescence or young adulthood; fire setting may increase or wax and wane; the course is unknown but smaller studies suggest it may be chronic."},
  {"id":"c18-ti01","tag":"Treatment","q":"A 28-year-old man with intermittent explosive disorder has punched walls and a coworker during brief rages over minor slights. He asks what treatment plan is most likely to work. Which approach has the best chance of success?","choices":["Psychotherapy alone","Combined medication and psychotherapy","Medication alone","Benzodiazepines as needed","Neurosurgical referral"],"why":"The chapter states that a combined pharmacologic and psychotherapeutic approach has the best chance of success in intermittent explosive disorder (Treatment Approach).","answer":1},
  {"id":"c18-ti02","tag":"Treatment","q":"A 19-year-old man who lives with his parents has intermittent explosive disorder with repeated outbursts at home. His parents are willing to take part in treatment. Which psychotherapy is especially useful at his age?","choices":["Supportive therapy alone","Psychoanalysis","Systematic desensitization","Family therapy","Aversive conditioning"],"why":"Family therapy is useful in intermittent explosive disorder, especially when the patient is an adolescent or a young adult (Treatment Approach).","answer":3},
  {"id":"c18-ti03","tag":"Treatment","q":"A 35-year-old woman with intermittent explosive disorder was started on a new medication. Over the next two weeks her outbursts became more frequent and less restrained. Which drug is most likely responsible?","choices":["Lithium","Lorazepam","Propranolol","Trazodone","Buspirone"],"why":"Benzodiazepines can cause disinhibition and so worsen aggressive behavior; lithium, buspirone, propranolol and trazodone are all reported to help (Treatment Approach).","answer":1},
  {"id":"c18-ti04","tag":"Treatment","q":"A 24-year-old man diagnosed with intermittent explosive disorder responds dramatically to an antipsychotic after several other drugs failed. What is the best next step?","choices":["Stop the antipsychotic because it cannot work in this disorder","Reconsider whether schizophrenia or a mood disorder is the correct diagnosis","Add a benzodiazepine to consolidate the response","Refer for neurosurgery","Confirm the diagnosis of intermittent explosive disorder and continue unchanged"],"why":"When antipsychotics or tricyclics are effective, clinicians must question whether schizophrenia or a mood disorder is the correct diagnosis (Treatment Approach).","answer":1},
  {"id":"c18-ti05","tag":"Treatment","q":"A 32-year-old man with intermittent explosive disorder wants a medication to reduce his impulsivity and aggression. He has a history of disinhibition with sedatives. Which option is best supported?","choices":["Clonazepam","Alprazolam","Diazepam","Buspirone","Lorazepam"],"why":"SSRIs, trazodone and buspirone are useful in reducing impulsivity and aggression; the benzodiazepines can cause disinhibition and worsen behavior (Treatment Approach).","answer":3},
  {"id":"c18-ti06","tag":"Treatment","q":"The family of a 40-year-old man with years of intractable violent outbursts asks whether brain surgery could cure him. What is the most accurate reply?","choices":["Surgery is effective but reserved for men","Surgery is proven effective when episodes exceed one per week","Some surgeons have operated, but there is no evidence it is effective","Surgery works only when combined with lithium","Surgery is the treatment of choice after two failed drugs"],"why":"Some neurosurgeons have performed operations for intractable violence and aggression, but no evidence indicates that such treatment is effective (Treatment Approach).","answer":2},
  {"id":"c18-ti07","tag":"Treatment","q":"A 27-year-old woman with intermittent explosive disorder is in weekly therapy. Her therapist asks her to describe, in detail, what she thought and felt just before each outburst. What is the main aim of this technique?","choices":["To have her verbalize the precursors of outbursts instead of acting them out","To document episodes for legal purposes","To uncover a hidden psychotic process","To provoke an outburst in session for exposure","To replace medication with self-monitoring"],"why":"A goal of treatment is for the patient to recognize and verbalize the thoughts or feelings that precede explosive outbursts instead of acting them out (Treatment Approach).","answer":0},
  {"id":"c18-ti08","tag":"Treatment","q":"A 33-year-old man with intermittent explosive disorder is on a drug that lowers the seizure threshold, and his outbursts have worsened. Why might this drug aggravate his disorder?","choices":["It blocks beta-adrenergic receptors","It increases testosterone","It raises CSF 5-HIAA","Subcortical seizure-like activity may be present in this disorder","It causes serotonin syndrome"],"why":"Because subcortical seizure-like activity may be present, medications that lower the seizure threshold can aggravate the situation (Treatment Approach).","answer":3},
  {"id":"c18-tk01","tag":"Treatment","q":"A 45-year-old woman with kleptomania feels intense guilt and shame after each theft and asks for help to stop. Which therapy is she especially likely to benefit from?","choices":["Electroconvulsive therapy first-line","Insight-oriented psychotherapy","Incarceration","Neurosurgery","Benzodiazepine therapy"],"why":"Patients who feel guilt and shame may be helped by insight-oriented psychotherapy because their motivation to change is increased (Treatment Approach, Kleptomania).","answer":1},
  {"id":"c18-tk02","tag":"Treatment","q":"A 30-year-old man with kleptomania is referred by the court and shows little motivation to change. Which treatment has been reported successful even when motivation is lacking?","choices":["Supportive listening alone","Insight-oriented psychotherapy","Psychoanalysis","Escitalopram","Aversive conditioning combined with altered social contingencies"],"why":"Specific behavior therapies, including aversive conditioning combined with altered social contingencies, have been reported successful even when motivation was lacking; insight-oriented work depends on motivation and escitalopram showed no benefit (Treatment Approach, Kleptomania).","answer":4},
  {"id":"c18-tk03","tag":"Treatment","q":"A resident reviews medication evidence for a 38-year-old woman with kleptomania. Which drug showed no benefit in a controlled trial of antidepressants?","choices":["High-dose naltrexone","Escitalopram","Fluvoxamine","Topiramate","Lithium"],"why":"In a controlled trial of antidepressants, escitalopram was found to have no benefit in kleptomania (Treatment Approach, Kleptomania).","answer":1},
  {"id":"c18-tk04","tag":"Treatment","q":"A 29-year-old man with kleptomania reports a constant urge to steal. Which treatment has been reported to reduce the urge to steal?","choices":["Phenytoin","High-dose naltrexone","Escitalopram","Olanzapine","Clonazepam"],"why":"High-dose naltrexone reduced the urge to steal in a small open-label study (Treatment Approach, Kleptomania), and a later small placebo-controlled trial also found benefit (update box).","answer":1},
  {"id":"c18-tk05","tag":"Treatment","q":"A 22-year-old man meets criteria for pyromania after setting several fires. Which treatment appears most promising?","choices":["Cognitive-behavioral therapy","Antiandrogen agents","Olanzapine","Clonazepam","Psychoanalysis"],"why":"No single treatment has been proved effective in pyromania, but among the treatments attempted, CBT appears the most promising; many drug reports found no benefit (Treatment Approach, Pyromania).","answer":0},
  {"id":"c18-tk06","tag":"Treatment","q":"A 9-year-old boy has set three small fires in his family's yard over two months. His parents ask how seriously to take this. What is the best approach?","choices":["Start olanzapine and avoid psychotherapy","Take it very seriously with intensive therapeutic intervention that includes family therapy","Use strict punishment as the main intervention","Treat the child alone without involving the family","Reassure the parents and review in a year"],"why":"Fire setting by children should be treated with the utmost seriousness, with intensive interventions as therapeutic and preventive measures, not punishment, and treatment of children and adolescents should include family therapy (Treatment Approach, Pyromania).","answer":1},
  {"id":"c18-tk07","tag":"Treatment","q":"A 26-year-old man with pyromania is starting outpatient therapy after his latest fire. Because the disorder is recurrent, which element must the treatment plan include?","choices":["Psychoanalysis as the sole treatment","Supervision to prevent another fire","Weekly urine drug screens only","A trial of escitalopram","Discharge once he expresses remorse"],"why":"Because pyromania is recurrent, any treatment program should include supervision to prevent a repeated episode of fire setting; incarceration may be the only way to avoid recurrence while therapy begins (Treatment Approach, Pyromania).","answer":1},
  {"id":"c18-tk08","tag":"Treatment","q":"A 41-year-old woman with kleptomania had no improvement after a full trial of fluoxetine. Which statement best describes the evidence for fluoxetine in kleptomania?","choices":["It is contraindicated because of disinhibition","It is proven effective in controlled trials","It works only with ECT","It has never been studied in kleptomania","It helps some patients, but other reports show no benefit"],"why":"SSRIs such as fluoxetine and fluvoxamine appear effective in some patients, but there are also reports of fluoxetine having no benefit in kleptomania (Treatment Approach, Kleptomania).","answer":4},
  {"id":"c18-ep01","tag":"Epidemiology","q":"A public health team plans a community survey to measure kleptomania and pyromania. What is the main expected source of error?","choices":["Underreporting because the behavior is usually illegal","Seasonal variation","Misclassification as dementia","Overreporting because the disorders are fashionable","Excess reporting by men"],"why":"All these disorders are underreported, understandably, because self-reporting usually illegal behavior has potential ramifications (Epidemiology).","answer":0},
  {"id":"c18-ep02","tag":"Epidemiology","q":"A psychiatrist compares patients with intermittent explosive disorder seen in a prison and in a psychiatric hospital. Which pattern would she expect?","choices":["Almost no cases in the prison","Only adolescents in both settings","Equal numbers of men and women in both","Mostly women in the prison and men in the hospital","Mostly men in the prison and women in the hospital"],"why":"Intermittent explosive disorder is more common in men; affected men are likely to be in correctional institutions and women in psychiatric facilities (Epidemiology).","answer":4},
  {"id":"c18-ep03","tag":"Epidemiology","q":"A 16-year-old boy is brought in after setting a fire in a school bin. His mother fears he has pyromania. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["Adolescents account for under 10 percent of US fire setters","Most adolescent fire setters do not meet criteria for pyromania","Most adolescent fire setters have pyromania","Pyromania has a known prevalence of 5 percent","Pyromania is more common in girls than boys"],"why":"In the United States, 60 percent of fire setters are adolescents, but most would not meet criteria for pyromania; the disorder is far more common in men and its prevalence is unknown (Epidemiology).","answer":1},
  {"id":"c18-ep04","tag":"Epidemiology","q":"A store security consultant asks what share of people arrested for shoplifting might have kleptomania. What range is reported?","choices":["Over 90 percent","0.1 to 0.5 percent","4 to 24 percent","60 to 80 percent","30 to 50 percent"],"why":"Kleptomania estimates range from 4 to 24 percent of those arrested for shoplifting (Epidemiology).","answer":2},
  {"id":"c18-ep05","tag":"Epidemiology","q":"A kleptomania specialty clinic reviews its caseload of 40 patients. Based on clinical samples, about how many would be women?","choices":["About 38","About 20","About 30","About 5","About 10"],"why":"The male-to-female ratio of kleptomania in clinical samples is 1:3, so about three quarters (about 30 of 40) would be women (Epidemiology).","answer":2},
  {"id":"c18-ep06","tag":"Epidemiology","q":"A university hospital psychiatric service reviews its admissions for intermittent explosive disorder, matching one published study. Which finding would match that study?","choices":["About 2 percent of admissions, mostly women","Under 0.1 percent of admissions, all men","About 20 percent of admissions, mostly women","About 8 percent of admissions, equal sexes","About 2 percent of admissions, 80 percent of them men"],"why":"In one study, about 2 percent of persons admitted to a university hospital psychiatric service had intermittent explosive disorder, and 80 percent were men (Epidemiology).","answer":4},
  {"id":"c18-ep07","tag":"Epidemiology","q":"A medical student preparing a talk asks for the estimated lifetime prevalence of intermittent explosive disorder. Which figure is correct?","choices":["About 60 percent","About 0.6 percent","About 4 to 24 percent","About 0.01 percent","About 5 to 8 percent"],"why":"The lifetime prevalence of intermittent explosive disorder is estimated at around 5 to 8 percent; 0.6 percent is the kleptomania estimate (Epidemiology).","answer":4},
  {"id":"c18-ei01","tag":"Etiology","q":"A 25-year-old man with intermittent explosive disorder was physically abused as a child. A study links his childhood abuse to the disorder. Which factors were found to mediate this relationship?","choices":["Anxiety and depression","Impulsivity and aggression","Psychosis and paranoia","Substance use and poverty","Low intelligence and poor schooling"],"why":"Physical abuse in childhood was independently associated with intermittent explosive disorder, with impulsivity and aggression as the mediators (Etiology, Intermittent Explosive Disorder).","answer":1},
  {"id":"c18-ei02","tag":"Etiology","q":"In an experiment, serotonin synthesis is blocked, and subjects keep repeating a behavior despite being punished for it. Which explanation fits best?","choices":["Dopamine excess blocks learning","Testosterone falls and removes inhibition","Raised serotonin increases fear of punishment","Norepinephrine rises and drives arousal","Reduced serotonin weakens punishment as a deterrent"],"why":"Decreased serotonergic transmission, by inhibiting synthesis or antagonizing its effects, reduces the impact of punishment as a deterrent; serotonergic neurons mediate behavioral inhibition (Etiology, Intermittent Explosive Disorder).","answer":4},
  {"id":"c18-ei03","tag":"Etiology","q":"A research lumbar puncture is done in a 34-year-old man with impulsive aggression and a past violent suicide attempt. Which CSF finding has been correlated with this picture?","choices":["High 5-HIAA","Low testosterone","Low 5-HIAA","High MHPG","Normal metabolites"],"why":"Low CSF 5-HIAA correlates with violent suicide attempts and impulsive aggression in some studies (Etiology, Intermittent Explosive Disorder).","answer":2},
  {"id":"c18-ei04","tag":"Etiology","q":"A 30-year-old man with repeated interpersonal violence is enrolled in a CSF study. Which hormonal finding is associated with aggressiveness in men?","choices":["High CSF prolactin","High CSF cortisol","Low CSF estrogen","High CSF testosterone","Low CSF testosterone"],"why":"High CSF testosterone concentrations are associated with aggressiveness and interpersonal violence in men, and antiandrogenic agents decrease aggression (Etiology, Intermittent Explosive Disorder).","answer":3},
  {"id":"c18-ei05","tag":"Etiology","q":"A 23-year-old man with intermittent explosive disorder says his father and brother have the same problem and asks if it is purely genetic. What is the best answer?","choices":["Only maternal relatives carry the risk","It is never seen in relatives","It is more common in first-degree relatives, but that does not prove a genetic cause","It is purely genetic and family factors do not matter","Family clustering rules out environmental causes"],"why":"Intermittent explosive disorder is more common in first-degree biologic relatives, but this does not imply a genetic etiology because familial and social factors are influential (Etiology, Intermittent Explosive Disorder).","answer":2},
  {"id":"c18-ei06","tag":"Etiology","q":"A 37-year-old nurse working repeated double shifts notices she loses her temper far more easily when exhausted. Which factor in her history is known to lower resistance to impulses?","choices":["Stable routine","Fatigue","Low stimulation","Regular exercise","Adequate sleep"],"why":"Fatigue, constant stimulation and psychic trauma can lower a person's resistance to controlling impulses (Etiology).","answer":1},
  {"id":"c18-ei07","tag":"Etiology","q":"A 20-year-old man carries a serotonin gene polymorphism linked to aggression and was maltreated as a child. Which model best explains his risk of impulsive aggression?","choices":["Purely genetic inheritance","Purely learned behavior","Toxic exposure","Endocrine tumor","Gene-environment interaction"],"why":"A gene-environment interaction may exist in which people with specific serotonin gene polymorphisms are more prone to impulsive aggression after childhood maltreatment (Etiology, Intermittent Explosive Disorder).","answer":4},
  {"id":"c18-ek01","tag":"Etiology","q":"A 58-year-old woman with no history of stealing begins taking small, useless items from shops. She also has spells of sweating and confusion with low blood glucose. Which condition reported with kleptomania should be sought?","choices":["Addison disease","Cushing syndrome","Pheochromocytoma","Hyperthyroidism","Insulinoma"],"why":"Case reports link kleptomania to hypoglycemia secondary to insulinoma, as well as head trauma, frontal lobe lesions, cortical atrophy and dementia (Etiology, Kleptomania).","answer":4},
  {"id":"c18-ek02","tag":"Etiology","q":"A 44-year-old man develops kleptomania after a car accident with a head injury. Lesions in which brain region have been reported with kleptomania?","choices":["Pituitary gland","Spinal cord","Frontal lobe","Occipital lobe","Cerebellum"],"why":"Kleptomania has been reported with head trauma and frontal lobe lesions, along with cortical atrophy, dementia and insulinoma (Etiology, Kleptomania).","answer":2},
  {"id":"c18-ek03","tag":"Etiology","q":"A research team scans adults with kleptomania using diffusion tensor imaging. What type of finding is suggested by such studies?","choices":["Normal brain in every respect","Enlarged pituitary","Calcified basal ganglia","Subtle white matter pathology","Large cortical infarcts"],"why":"Diffusion tensor imaging results suggest subtle white matter pathology in kleptomania (Etiology, Kleptomania).","answer":3},
  {"id":"c18-ek04","tag":"Etiology","q":"A 36-year-old woman with severe, long-standing kleptomania undergoes neuropsychological testing. Which pattern has been reported?","choices":["Group scores normal, but greater severity linked to executive impairment","Global intellectual decline in all patients","Better executive function with greater severity","Severe language deficits in all patients","Marked memory loss in all patients"],"why":"As a group, patients with kleptomania did not show deficits on neuropsychological testing, but severity of illness was associated with impaired executive functioning (Etiology, Kleptomania).","answer":0},
  {"id":"c18-ek05","tag":"Etiology","q":"A 31-year-old man with kleptomania gives a family history. Which disorder is more common in his first-degree relatives than in controls?","choices":["Autism spectrum disorder","Alcohol use disorder","Anorexia nervosa","Schizophrenia","Parkinson disease"],"why":"One study found more first-degree relatives with alcohol use disorders in people with kleptomania than in controls; relatives also have higher rates of OCD (Etiology, Kleptomania).","answer":1},
  {"id":"c18-ek06","tag":"Etiology","q":"A 27-year-old man with pyromania joins a biochemical study. Which finding has limited research suggested?","choices":["Higher MHPG and 5-HIAA","High CSF testosterone","Normal metabolites in all patients","Lower MHPG and 5-HIAA","High dopamine metabolites"],"why":"Limited studies suggest pyromania may be associated with lower concentrations of the norepinephrine and serotonin metabolites MHPG and 5-HIAA (Etiology, Pyromania).","answer":3},
  {"id":"c18-ek07","tag":"Etiology","q":"A 24-year-old man with pyromania also has a history of heavy drug use. Some authors propose a shared neurobiologic link between pyromania and which group of disorders?","choices":["Eating disorders","Psychotic disorders","Substance use disorders","Sleep disorders","Dementias"],"why":"Some authors suggest pyromania and the other impulse-control disorders may share a neurobiologic link with substance use disorders (Etiology, Pyromania).","answer":2}
]);
